The code statements that lead to the bug are:
static void f2fs_record_errors(struct f2fs_sb_info *sbi, unsigned char error) {
	f2fs_down_write(&sbi->sb_lock);
	err = f2fs_commit_super(sbi, false);
	f2fs_up_write(&sbi->sb_lock);
}
void f2fs_handle_error(struct f2fs_sb_info *sbi, unsigned char error) {
	f2fs_save_errors(sbi, error);
	f2fs_record_errors(sbi, error);
}
Reason:
These functions acquire `sb_lock` and call `f2fs_commit_super()` synchronously. The commit can trigger memory reclaim (`fs_reclaim`), which may re‑enter filesystem locks (`sb_internal`, `cp_rwsem`) already held, causing a circular deadlock as shown in the report.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
void f2fs_handle_error(struct f2fs_sb_info *sbi, unsigned char error) {
	f2fs_save_errors(sbi, error);
	if (!sbi->error_dirty)
		return;
	if (!test_bit(error, (unsigned long *)sbi->errors))
		return;
	schedule_work(&sbi->s_error_work);
}
Removal of `f2fs_record_errors()` and `f2fs_update_errors()`.
Reason:
The new `f2fs_handle_error()` only saves errors and schedules an asynchronous workqueue, avoiding the synchronous `sb_lock` acquisition and superblock commit. This breaks the deadlock chain by eliminating the path that could trigger memory reclaim while holding filesystem locks.

